//! `uniwidged completions <shell>` — the command line, as the shell sees it.
//!
//! Twenty-eight commands, and half of them take the name of a widget that is
//! installed on this machine. Typing `uniwidged configure sys<Tab>` and
//! getting `system-load` is the difference between a tool people use from the
//! terminal and one they use from a script they wrote once.
//!
//! ## Generated by the binary, not shipped as files
//!
//! Three scripts for three shells is three copies of the command list, and the
//! command list changes. So the scripts are written *from* a table here, by
//! the binary that implements the commands, and the package build asks the
//! binary for them — see `packaging/deb/build.sh`. A command added to `main`
//! without a row in [`COMMANDS`] fails a test rather than quietly having no
//! completion.
//!
//! ## What a widget name completes to
//!
//! `uniwidged list --names` prints one installed name per line and nothing
//! else, and the scripts call it. That is the only dynamic part: the shell
//! asks the binary, against the same `UNIWIDGED_CONFIG` the user's command will
//! run under, so what completes is what would work.

/// What a command's arguments look like, for the shell's benefit.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum Takes {
    /// Flags only, or nothing.
    Nothing,
    /// The name of an installed widget.
    Widget,
    /// A `.wasm` file.
    Component,
    /// One of a fixed set of words — `registry add`, `autostart enable`.
    Subcommand(&'static [&'static str]),
    /// A shell.
    Shell,
    /// A public key, which nothing can complete.
    Key,
}

/// One command, as `main` dispatches it.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]
pub struct Command {
    pub name: &'static str,
    pub summary: &'static str,
    pub takes: Takes,
    /// The flags it understands, `--config` excepted: every command takes
    /// that, and it is added once below.
    pub flags: &'static [&'static str],
}

